---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/site/CopyPrompt.astro';
import { models } from '../../data/models';
import { t3code } from '../../data/t3code';
import { ATTACK, STOP } from '../../journey/chapters/review';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';

// Who builds and who reviews. Swap the two keys and the lesson swaps the names; nothing else in
// the script depends on which model is which.
const builder = models['claude-opus-5-5'];
const reviewer = models['gpt-6-2-sol'];
const short = (name: string) => name.replace(/^Claude /, '');
const data = {
  builder: short(builder.name), reviewer: short(reviewer.name),
  builderLab: builder.lab, reviewerLab: reviewer.lab,
  handoffCap: t3code.handoffCap.value,
};
const ids = [
  'harnesses.claudeCode.codeReview', 'harnesses.claudeCode.sameModelReviewer', 'harnesses.claudeCode.reviewerAlwaysFinds', 'plans.claude.pro.claudeCode', 'plans.chatgpt.limits.codexOnEveryPlan',
  't3code.handoff', 't3code.handoffCap', 'harnesses.codex.sandbox',
];
---
<Journey chapter="review" first="self" data={data} title="A second model" description="One model builds, a different model reviews in a fresh window, and they go round until the real flaws are gone.">
  <Step id="self" title="Asked to check itself, it trusts its own work">
    <p>{data.builder} builds a made-up feature with three planted flaws, and the tests it wrote pass <a href={url('journey/check/')}>the check from Make it prove it</a>. Asked to review its own work, it reads back through the same window, with the same assumptions, and finds one.</p>
  </Step>
  <Step id="second" title="A different model, an empty window">
    <p>Now {data.reviewer}, a model from {data.reviewerLab}, reviews it in a fresh window that holds only your request and the change. Nothing of the builder’s reasoning comes with it, and it is a different model with different habits: findings go back, get fixed, and the rounds stop when one comes back clean.</p>
    <More title="Where this comes from">
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.sameModelReviewer" show="claim" /> <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.codeReview" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="attack" title="Tell it to try to break it">
    <p>What you ask for is what you get. Compare a polite request with one that asks for the ways it fails and how to see each one.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="brief" data-val="polite" data-default aria-pressed="true">“Review this.”</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="brief" data-val="attack" aria-pressed="false">“Find the three ways it is wrong”</button>
    </div>
    <p>“{ATTACK}”</p>
    <More title="The whole prompt, to copy">
      <CopyPrompt title="Ask a second model for an adversarial review" compact>{`You are reviewing a change you did not write. You get the task and the diff below, and nothing else: do not ask for the author's explanation.

Find the three most likely ways this change is wrong: a case it mishandles, someone it lets do too much, data it breaks. For each, give a way to see it fail: a command, a test to write, or steps to follow in the app. Skip style and naming. If you find fewer than three real problems, say so.

Task: <paste the task you gave the first model>

Diff: <paste the output of git diff>`}</CopyPrompt>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="rounds" title="Say when to stop">
    <p>Each model runs on its own plan, and every round uses some of that plan’s allowance: the share of use you get before it pauses (see <a href={url('journey/meter/')}>What it costs</a>). Without a rule the reviewer always finds something more; with one, it stops.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="rule" data-val="none" data-default aria-pressed="true">No limit</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="rule" data-val="rule" aria-pressed="false">“{STOP}”</button>
    </div>
    <More title="Where this comes from">
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.reviewerAlwaysFinds" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="how" title="Three ways to run the loop">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Paste the findings between two threads yourself, hand one thread from one model to the other, or let <a href={url('journey/lead/')}>the lead from A lead</a> run the rounds and bring you one report.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>By hand, the reviewer gets exactly what you paste: the task and <code>git diff</code>, plus the test output if you want it checked. A handoff passes a selection of the thread; a lead runs the rounds and reports once.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>In a team this loop runs before a pull request is opened, so a colleague reads a change that has already been attacked. By hand keeps the reviewer’s window cleanest; a handoff is faster but less independent; a lead costs a third window but none of your attention.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="hand" data-default aria-pressed="true">By hand</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="handoff" aria-pressed="false">One thread, handed off</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="how" data-val="lead" aria-pressed="false">A lead runs the loop</button>
    </div>
    <More title="In T3 Code and Codex">
      <p><Fact id="t3code.handoff" show="claim" /> The selection holds <Fact id="t3code.handoffCap" /> by default. <Fact id="harnesses.codex.sandbox" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
    <Next from="review" more={[{ href: 'explain/verification/', text: 'Checking the work' }, { href: 'models/', text: 'The models' }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The feature, its three flaws, the review comments and how fast the allowances drain are made up. Which model builds and which reviews can be swapped; what matters is that they are different. <Fact id="plans.claude.pro.claudeCode" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.chatgpt.limits.codexOnEveryPlan" show="claim" /></p>
    <Verified ids={ids} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
